  "notes": "iṣya (1 sg. pres. of √iṣ 'send') is an irregular form; upastire is unclear (taken as 'at the spreading', perhaps of the sacred grass; the text does not say). pari ... babhūvuḥ taken as 'encompassed'. evaiḥ = 'courses' (eva m.). apasaḥ is the adjective 'doers of work' (apas 'work'). 'Those who' relates to the Ṛbhus of the first line.",
